@startuml sre-telemetry-topology
' ============================================================================
'  agentd SRE / Observability — the plan
'
'  Page 1  topology        where telemetry is emitted and how it reaches CloudWatch
'  Page 2  correlation id  one ID from keystroke to billing row
'  Page 3  the four clocks what "time to first token" actually means here
'  Page 4  module anatomy  what in v2/monitoring/ is a LIBRARY vs a SERVICE
'  Page 5  build order     phases, effort, payoff
'
'  Location tags are the same as the request-flow diagrams:
'    (USER PC) = the user's machine    (ON AWS) = our fleet
'
'  Render (from this folder):
'    java -jar ../../../plantuml.jar -tpng sre-observability.puml
' ============================================================================

title Observability topology — ONE library, TWO roads, ONE destination

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam TitleFontSize 17
skinparam package {
  BackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  BorderColor #888888
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBE6
  BorderColor #C8B900
}

package "THE LIBRARY — v2/monitoring/telemetry/   (imported, never deployed)" as LIB #F0FFF0 {
  rectangle "count()  timer()  log()  redact()\n--\nEvery call just PRINTS one line of JSON.\nPrinting cannot fail, cannot be slow,\nand has no dependency." as TEL #DFF5DF
}

package "DESKTOP CLOUD MODE   (USER PC)" as PC #EAF3FF {
  rectangle "agentd daemon\nimports the library" as DD
  rectangle "Electron shell" as SH
  rectangle "local log file\n~/.agentd/logs\n**we can NEVER read this**" as LOG #FFE0E0
  rectangle "uploader\nbatch + consent + redact\n(6 numbers per run, no content)" as UP #FFF3CD
}

package "CLOUD WEB MODE   (ON AWS)" as WEBMODE #E8F5E9 {
  rectangle "agentd daemon\n<<ECS task>>\nimports the library" as CD
  rectangle "web UI\n(browser JS)" as WEB
}

package "SHARED BY BOTH MODES   (ON AWS)" as SHARED #FFF0F0 {
  rectangle "Model Proxy\n<<ECS task>>\nimports the library" as MP
  rectangle "Accounts\n<<ECS task>>\nimports the library" as AC
  rectangle "**ingest**\n<<ECS task — the ONLY new service>>\nPOST /v1/events -> validate -> print" as ING #FFD9D9
}

database "CloudWatch Logs + Metrics\n(the awslogs driver at services.tf:46-53\nALREADY does this — no new infra)" as CW #E0F2F1

TEL ..> DD  : import
TEL ..> CD  : import
TEL ..> MP  : import
TEL ..> AC  : import
TEL ..> ING : import

DD --> LOG : print()  ->  dead end
DD --> UP  : agent_end summary
SH --> UP  : crash / start-failure
UP --> ING : HTTPS  (the ONLY way off the user's PC)
WEB --> ING : browser errors + timings

CD --> CW : print() -> awslogs
MP --> CW : print() -> awslogs
AC --> CW : print() -> awslogs
ING --> CW : print() -> awslogs

note bottom of ING
  **Why this service exists at all.**
  A program's output lands where the program runs. The desktop daemon
  runs on the user's laptop, so its printed lines are on THEIR disk and
  unreachable. The app must mail us a summary; ingest is the mailbox.
  Everything on AWS needs no mailbox — it just prints.
end note

note right of CW
  **Both roads end here.** Ingest is not a
  separate monitoring system — it only gets
  desktop/browser data onto our servers so it
  can join the same pipe everything else uses.
end note

legend left
  | Signal | Measured in | Desktop Cloud | Cloud Web |
  | ledger write success | Model Proxy | complete | complete |
  | token resolve ok + latency | Model Proxy | complete | complete |
  | cost per hour / per account | Model Proxy | complete | complete |
  | time to first chunk | Model Proxy | complete | complete |
  | model call success | Model Proxy | complete | complete |
  | run / tool / turn metrics | daemon | uploader only | complete |
  | daemon start, version, connect | desktop shell | uploader only | n/a |
  | runs killed by deploy | daemon | n/a | complete |
endlegend

@enduml


@startuml sre-correlation-id
title Correlation ID — one ID from keystroke to billing row

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam TitleFontSize 17
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #444444
  LifeLineBorderColor #888888
  ParticipantBackgroundColor White
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBE6
  BorderColor #C8B900
}

participant "Client\nstore.ts"                as C
participant "Gateway\ngateway.py"             as G
participant "Loop\nnative.py"                 as L
participant "proxy seam\nmodel_proxy.py"      as S
participant "Model Proxy\ncustom_auth.py"     as P
participant "Accounts\napp.py"                as A
database    "CloudWatch"                      as CW

== born at the EDGE, not inside the server ==

C -> C : traceId = crypto.randomUUID()
note right of C
  TODAY store.ts:1278 sends only idempotencyKey
  (sessionKey + timestamp) — for dedup, not tracing.
end note
C -> G : chat.send { traceId, sessionKey, message, ... }

G -> G : run_id = traceId  (else generate)
note right of G
  TODAY gateway.py:3905 always generates
  uuid4().hex[:12] — born at line 3905, so the
  55 lines BEFORE it have no ID at all:
  validation, attachment save, idempotency,
  active-run check (3903 RAISES).
  Widen to full uuid4: it becomes a ledger join key.
end note

G -> CW : log { run_id, event: "run_start" }
G -> L  : start run

== the ID rides in a contextvar, not in 200 signatures ==

loop one model turn
  L -> L : turn_id = run_id + "-" + iterations
  L -> S : litellm kwargs
  S -> S : extra_headers\nX-Agentd-Run-Id / X-Agentd-Turn-Id
  note right of S
    model_proxy.apply() already rewrites
    model + api_base + api_key. Two more fields.
  end note
  S -> P : POST /chat/completions  (+ headers)
  P -> CW : log { run_id, turn_id, ttfb_ms, model, outcome }
  P -> A  : POST /usage { run_id, account_id, tokens, cost }
  A -> A  : store run_id on the usage row
  A -> CW : log { run_id, event: "usage_written" }
end

L -> G  : agent_end
G -> CW : log { run_id, total_ms, model_ms, tool_ms, turns, outcome }

note over C, CW
  **Payoff.** A user complains -> search ONE id ->
  the message, every model call, every timing,
  every error, and exactly what it cost.
  Without it, "my chat broke at 3pm" is unanswerable.
end note

note over C, A
  **A hierarchy, not one ID:**
  account_id  = who            (from /resolve)
  session_key = conversation   (exists)
  run_id      = one user action  <-- THE correlation id
  turn_id     = one model turn
  X-Amzn-Trace-Id = the ALB's own id — log it in web mode to
  join ALB access logs (502s, timeouts) to app logs. Free.
end note

@enduml


@startuml sre-four-clocks
title The four clocks — "time to first token" is FOUR different numbers

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam TitleFontSize 17
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #444444
  LifeLineBorderColor #888888
  ParticipantBackgroundColor White
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBE6
  BorderColor #C8B900
}

actor       "User"                       as U
participant "Gateway"                    as G
participant "Loop\nnative.py"            as L
participant "Model Proxy\n(ON AWS)"      as P
participant "Provider"                   as V
participant "Tool\n(local)"              as T

U -> G  : T0  hits enter
G -> U  : T1  res { runId }   (ack only — the answer never comes back here)
G -> L  : T2  agent_start                      native.py:170
L -> L  : T3  turn_start                       native.py:180

== CLOCK 1 — infra latency (the only one WE fully control) ==
L -> P  : T4  request LEAVES the machine
P -> P  : T5  auth + /resolve                  custom_auth.py:85
P -> V  : forward with OUR provider key
V --> P : T6  first chunk
P --> L : first chunk
note right of P
  **CLOCK 1 = T4 -> T6.** Auth + resolve + proxy + provider queue.
  Measured AT THE PROXY, so it is complete in BOTH modes.
  300ms = we are fine, slowness is the model.
  3s = our problem, probably /resolve.
end note

== CLOCK 2 — does it feel alive ==
L -> U  : T7  first thinking_delta rendered    native.py:200-203
note right of L
  **CLOCK 2 = T0 -> T7.** The UI streams thinking, so this is
  usually the first thinking_delta. Under 1s feels instant,
  over 3s feels broken — even if the full answer is faster.
end note

== the loop: this is why one message != one call ==
L -> T  : toolcall_end -> run tool LOCALLY     litellm.py:410
T --> L : ToolResultMessage
note right of T
  **model_time vs tool_time.** A 90s run:
  80 model / 10 tool  -> the provider is slow
  10 model / 80 tool  -> OUR code is slow
  30 / 10 + 50 missing -> a bug (the loop is stalling)
end note
L -> P  : turn 2 ...   (metered again)

== CLOCK 3 — when did they get REAL content ==
L -> U  : T9  first text_delta of the FINAL turn   litellm.py:380
note right of L
  **CLOCK 3 = T0 -> T9.** text_delta also fires on intermediate
  turns ("let me check that..."), so you only know WHICH turn was
  final once the loop ends — compute this retroactively at agent_end.
end note

== CLOCK 4 — total ==
L -> G  : T10 agent_end                        native.py:345
G -> U  : final answer, session idle

note over U, V
  **Which token did you mean? All three — they blame different people.**
  first THINKING token -> the pipe works, auth passed        (us / provider)
  TOOL execution time  -> our code, our disk, our network    (US)
  first ANSWER token   -> when the user got real value       (agent design)
  Track only one and the other two hide.
end note

legend left
  | Clock | Span | Measured in | Desktop Cloud | Cloud Web |
  | 1 infra latency | T4 -> T6 | Model Proxy | complete | complete |
  | 2 feels alive | T0 -> T7 | daemon | uploader only | complete |
  | 3 answer start | T0 -> T9 | daemon | uploader only | complete |
  | 4 total | T0 -> T10 | daemon | uploader only | complete |
endlegend

@enduml


@startuml sre-module-anatomy
title v2/monitoring/ — what is a LIBRARY, what is a SERVICE

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam TitleFontSize 17
skinparam package {
  BackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  BorderColor #888888
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBE6
  BorderColor #C8B900
}

package "v2/monitoring/" as M {

  package "NOT PROGRAMS — imported or applied" as NOTP #F0FFF0 {
    rectangle "**telemetry/**\ncount() timer() log() redact()\nimported by daemon + accounts + model_proxy\n(like utils.py — nothing runs it)" as T1
    rectangle "**terraform/**\nalarms + dashboards + synthetics schedule\nCALLED FROM infra/environments/dev/main.tf\n(owned here, applied there)" as T2
    rectangle "**runbooks/**\nmarkdown. one per alarm.\nlinked from the alarm description" as T3
    rectangle "**synthetics/**\ncanary script: login -> chat ->\nassert stream -> assert ledger row" as T4
  }

  package "THE ONE REAL SERVICE" as ISP #FFF0F0 {
    rectangle "**ingest/**\nFastAPI, POST /v1/events\nschema allowlist + rate limit -> print()\n<<deploys as an ECS task>>" as T5 #FFD9D9
  }
}

rectangle "agent_runtime\n(daemon)"   as D
rectangle "accounts"                  as A
rectangle "model_proxy"               as P
rectangle "infra/environments/dev"    as I
database  "CloudWatch"                as CW

T1 ..> D : import
T1 ..> A : import
T1 ..> P : import
T2 <.. I : module "../../monitoring/terraform"
T5 --> CW : print()
T4 ..> T2 : scheduled by

note bottom of T5
  Adding this service = ONE entry in the services map at
  infra/modules/variables.tf. The existing for_each in
  services.tf then builds the task, target group, listener
  and DNS automatically. Infra is data-driven already.
end note

note right of T1
  **Never build a monitoring SERVICE that apps call.**
  It puts a network hop in the hot path, and you lose
  telemetry exactly when things break — the thing that
  reports incidents is on the far side of the outage.
  Apps write to stdout, which cannot fail.
  (custom_auth.py:149 is this failure mode today.)
end note

note bottom of T2
  Industry shape, unchanged since Borgmon:
  a LIBRARY in the app -> something NEARBY that ships it -> a
  BIG SYSTEM far away that stores and graphs it.
  At our scale, awslogs IS the "nearby" part, already running.
end note

@enduml


@startuml sre-build-order
title Build order — phases, and what each one buys

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam TitleFontSize 17
skinparam package {
  BackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  BorderColor #888888
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBE6
  BorderColor #C8B900
}

rectangle "**0 · FOUNDATION**\n1 monitoring library (telemetry/)\n--\nunblocks everything" as P0 #DFF5DF

rectangle "**1 · TRACEABILITY**\n2 correlation id (client -> ledger)\n3 structured JSON logging\n--\nwe can debug a complaint" as P1 #DCEBFF

rectangle "**2 · MONEY + TIMING**\n4 proxy instrumentation\n5 fix silent ledger loss\n6 enforce budgets at the proxy\n7 the four clocks\n--\n**stops real losses. highest value.**" as P2 #FFE0B2

rectangle "**3 · ALERTING**\n8 alarms + dashboards\n9 deep health check (/health/ready)\n10 synthetic canary\n--\nwe learn before users tell us" as P3 #E1BEE7

rectangle "**4 · HOSTED SAFETY**\n11 SIGTERM graceful shutdown\n12 deployment circuit breaker\n--\ndeploys stop killing live chats" as P4 #FFCDD2

rectangle "**5 · CLIENT REACH**\n13 desktop uploader (+consent)\n14 ingest service\n15 web RUM\n16 runbooks\n--\ndesktop visibility. most expensive." as P5 #CFD8DC

P0 --> P1
P1 --> P2
P2 --> P3
P3 --> P4
P4 --> P5

note right of P2
  Phases 1-3 cover desktop AND web **equally** —
  they live in the Model Proxy and Accounts, which are
  ON AWS in both modes. Maximum coverage, least work.
  Phase 5 is the only desktop-specific part, and the
  priciest, which is why it is last.
end note

note bottom of P4
  **Two live defects found during this review — neither is "monitoring":**
  1. custom_auth.py:149 swallows failed billing writes. Accounts down
     for an hour = money spent, nothing recorded, no symptom.
  2. budget_usd is enforced NOWHERE in desktop Cloud mode: the daemon
     check at gateway.py:3874 needs accounts.enabled() (false on desktop)
     and the proxy never checks at all.
  Plus: main.py:32 catches KeyboardInterrupt but ECS sends SIGTERM,
  so today every deploy kills every in-flight conversation.
end note

@enduml
